NAD JARVIS
Personal AI Assistant — Master Development Specification & Implementation Prompt
________________________________________
IMPORTANT INSTRUCTION TO GOOGLE ANTIGRAVITY
You are the primary development agent responsible for designing, implementing, integrating, testing, debugging, and refining NAD JARVIS, a private personal AI assistant.
Do not merely explain how to build the application.
Build the application.
You must inspect the existing project structure before making changes, understand the current implementation, preserve working functionality, and progressively implement the requirements described in this specification.
When a requirement is technically ambiguous, choose a robust, maintainable, production-ready implementation rather than creating a superficial mockup.
Do not replace functional systems unnecessarily.
Do not create fake functionality where a real implementation is technically possible.
Every major feature should be connected to the appropriate backend, database, API, authentication, storage, or AI service.
The final application should feel like a real personal AI operating system rather than a simple chatbot interface.
________________________________________
1. PROJECT IDENTITY
Name
NAD JARVIS
Description
NAD JARVIS is a private, voice-first personal AI assistant designed exclusively for one user.
It is inspired by the concept of J.A.R.V.I.S. from the Marvel universe, but it must be implemented as an original product with its own visual identity, architecture, interface, and functionality.
JARVIS should function as a personal digital companion capable of:
•	Natural conversation
•	Reasoning
•	Planning
•	Scheduling
•	Reminders
•	Voice interaction
•	Personal memory
•	Personal knowledge management
•	Document understanding
•	File management
•	Calendar management
•	Notifications
•	Web research
•	AI-powered task execution
•	Multi-step workflows
•	Personal productivity assistance
The application is intended to be private and personalized to a single user.
________________________________________
2. CORE DESIGN PHILOSOPHY
Do not build NAD JARVIS as:
"A chatbot with a dashboard."
Build it as:
"A personal AI operating system with an AI brain, persistent memory, a knowledge library, modular skills, secure integrations, and an action orchestration layer."
The architecture should separate:
1.	AI reasoning
2.	Memory
3.	Knowledge
4.	Skills/tools
5.	Integrations
6.	Permissions
7.	Data storage
8.	User interface
9.	Voice interaction
10.	Task execution
The AI model itself should not be tightly coupled to the application's core business logic.
This allows the underlying AI provider to be changed in the future without rebuilding the entire application.
________________________________________
3. PRIMARY USER
NAD JARVIS is designed primarily for one authenticated user.
The system should therefore prioritize:
•	Personalization
•	Privacy
•	Long-term memory
•	Personal knowledge
•	Personal projects
•	Personal files
•	Personal workflows
•	Secure authentication
•	Fine-grained permissions
Do not build unnecessary multi-tenant complexity unless the architecture requires it.
However, structure the database and security rules cleanly enough that expansion to multiple users could be possible later.
________________________________________
4. AI BRAIN
NAD JARVIS should support an AI orchestration architecture rather than hard-coding all functionality into a single model.
The original architecture specifies:
Gemini
Use Gemini for:
•	Complex reasoning
•	Structured tasks
•	Function/tool calling
•	Multi-step planning
•	Action execution
•	Scheduling
•	Tool selection
•	Knowledge retrieval orchestration
•	Complex document-related reasoning
DeepSeek
Use DeepSeek where appropriate for:
•	General conversation
•	Casual questions
•	Explanations
•	Brainstorming
•	Creative text generation
•	Cost-efficient conversational workloads
Do not assume that every request must go to the same model.
Create a central AI orchestration layer that determines which model and tools are appropriate for each request.
________________________________________
5. AI ORCHESTRATOR
Create a central JARVIS Orchestrator.
Its responsibility is to determine:
1.	What does the user want?
2.	Is this a conversational request?
3.	Does it require memory?
4.	Does it require knowledge retrieval?
5.	Does it require a skill/tool?
6.	Which skill should be executed?
7.	Does the action require confirmation?
8.	Which AI model should process the request?
9.	Does the task require multiple steps?
10.	What information should be returned to the user?
11.	Should the result be stored in memory?
Conceptually:
USER
  ↓
VOICE / TEXT
  ↓
JARVIS ORCHESTRATOR
  ↓
┌───────────────┬────────────────┬────────────────┐
│               │                │
AI BRAIN      MEMORY         KNOWLEDGE
│               │                │
│               │                │
└───────────────┴────────────────┘
                ↓
          SKILL ROUTER
                ↓
        TOOL / API EXECUTION
                ↓
             RESULT
                ↓
          AI RESPONSE
                ↓
        MEMORY / HISTORY
________________________________________
6. PERSONAL KNOWLEDGE LIBRARY
This is a major core feature of NAD JARVIS.
JARVIS must have a persistent Knowledge Library where the user can provide documents and other knowledge sources for future use.
The user should be able to tell JARVIS:
"Learn this document."
JARVIS must then process the document and make its information available for future retrieval.
IMPORTANT:
Do NOT interpret "learn" as retraining or fine-tuning the AI model every time a document is uploaded.
Instead, implement a persistent document knowledge architecture based on:
Document Storage + Metadata + Text Extraction + Chunking + Retrieval
________________________________________
7. FIREBASE STORAGE — ORIGINAL KNOWLEDGE FILES
Use Firebase Storage to store the actual uploaded knowledge documents.
Firebase Storage is the physical document repository.
Example:
knowledge/
│
├── filmmaking/
│   ├── celtx-screenwriting-handbook.pdf
│   ├── cinematography-guide.pdf
│   └── lighting-manual.pdf
│
├── engineering/
│   ├── mechanical-engineering-notes.pdf
│   └── final-year-project.pdf
│
├── programming/
│   ├── html-handbook.pdf
│   ├── css-guide.pdf
│   └── javascript-book.pdf
│
└── business/
    ├── seo-guide.pdf
    └── marketing-handbook.pdf
The original uploaded file must remain available in Firebase Storage.
Do not store large binary documents directly inside Firestore.
________________________________________
8. FIRESTORE — KNOWLEDGE DATABASE
Use Firestore as the structured knowledge management layer.
Firestore should contain metadata and information necessary to manage and retrieve documents.
A knowledge document record should contain information such as:
{
  id: "documentId",

  name: "Celtx Screenwriting Handbook",

  fileName: "celtx-screenwriting-handbook.pdf",

  storagePath:
    "knowledge/filmmaking/celtx-screenwriting-handbook.pdf",

  downloadUrl: "...",

  mimeType: "application/pdf",

  category: "Filmmaking",

  projectId: "nad-production",

  description: "...",

  uploadedAt: "...",

  updatedAt: "...",

  processingStatus: "completed",

  pageCount: 84,

  fileSize: 12345678,

  ownerId: "...",

  isEnabled: true
}
The exact schema may be improved by the implementation agent where appropriate.
________________________________________
9. KNOWLEDGE DOCUMENT PROCESSING
When a document is uploaded:
USER UPLOADS DOCUMENT
        ↓
FIREBASE STORAGE
        ↓
DOCUMENT PROCESSOR
        ↓
FILE TYPE DETECTION
        ↓
TEXT EXTRACTION
        ↓
STRUCTURAL ANALYSIS
        ↓
CHUNKING
        ↓
SEARCHABLE KNOWLEDGE
        ↓
FIRESTORE METADATA
        ↓
READY FOR JARVIS
Support appropriate document formats such as:
•	PDF
•	DOC
•	DOCX
•	TXT
•	Markdown
•	PPT/PPTX
•	XLS/XLSX where technically appropriate
•	Images through OCR where implemented
•	Other formats where reliable extraction is available
The implementation should gracefully reject unsupported formats.
________________________________________
10. DOCUMENT CHUNKING
Large documents must not be sent to the AI model in their entirety for every question.
Extract the document into meaningful sections/chunks.
Each chunk should preserve useful metadata such as:
{
  documentId: "...",

  text: "...",

  pageNumber: 42,

  sectionTitle: "Scene Headings",

  chunkIndex: 17,

  tokenCount: 450
}
Chunking should attempt to preserve semantic boundaries instead of blindly splitting text at arbitrary character counts.
________________________________________
11. KNOWLEDGE RETRIEVAL / RAG
Implement a retrieval architecture so JARVIS can answer questions using the user's stored documents.
Example:
User:
"What does my screenwriting handbook say about scene headings?"
Processing:
USER QUESTION
      ↓
QUERY ANALYSIS
      ↓
KNOWLEDGE SEARCH
      ↓
RELEVANT DOCUMENT CHUNKS
      ↓
CONTEXT ASSEMBLY
      ↓
AI MODEL
      ↓
ANSWER
JARVIS should retrieve only the relevant portions of the user's documents.
Do not unnecessarily send entire books or large documents to the AI.
Where technically appropriate, use vector/semantic search or another reliable retrieval mechanism.
If a vector-search service is introduced, keep it modular so the application can change providers later.
________________________________________
12. SOURCE CITATIONS
Whenever JARVIS answers a question using stored knowledge, it should be capable of identifying the source.
For example:
According to your Celtx Screenwriting Handbook,
scene headings identify the location and time of a scene.

Source:
Celtx Screenwriting Handbook
Page 42
The user should be able to select:
"View Source"
and JARVIS should open or display the relevant document section where technically possible.
This is important for trust and verification.
________________________________________
13. KNOWLEDGE VS MEMORY
Do not treat personal memory and document knowledge as the same system.
PERSONAL MEMORY
Memory contains information about the user.
Examples:
User prefers black and gold website designs.

User prefers voice interaction.

User is working on a project called X.

User prefers a particular workflow.
Memory capabilities:
memory.save()
memory.search()
memory.update()
memory.delete()
KNOWLEDGE
Knowledge contains information obtained from documents and approved knowledge sources.
Examples:
HTML handbook
Mechanical Engineering textbook
Celtx handbook
Production manual
Business guide
Project documentation
Knowledge capabilities:
knowledge.upload()
knowledge.process()
knowledge.search()
knowledge.retrieve()
knowledge.list()
knowledge.delete()
knowledge.update()
Keep these systems logically separate.
________________________________________
14. KNOWLEDGE ORGANIZATION
The Knowledge Library must support categories and projects.
Example:
KNOWLEDGE
│
├── FILMMAKING
│   ├── Screenwriting
│   ├── Cinematography
│   ├── Lighting
│   ├── Editing
│   └── AI Filmmaking
│
├── ENGINEERING
│   ├── Mechanical Engineering
│   ├── Robotics
│   ├── MATLAB
│   └── Final Year Project
│
├── PROGRAMMING
│   ├── HTML
│   ├── CSS
│   ├── JavaScript
│   ├── React
│   └── Firebase
│
└── BUSINESS
    ├── SEO
    ├── Marketing
    └── Entrepreneurship
Allow documents to belong to:
•	Global Knowledge
•	Personal Knowledge
•	Project Knowledge
•	Category-specific Knowledge
________________________________________
15. KNOWLEDGE SCOPES
Implement knowledge scopes.
Global
Available throughout JARVIS.
Personal
Available to the authenticated user.
Project
Available only when working with that project.
Category
Available to related tasks.
For example:
Project:
NAD PRODUCTION

Knowledge:
Celtx Handbook
Production Manual
Lighting Guide
Camera Guide
When the user asks:
"How should we format this screenplay?"
JARVIS should be able to use the relevant filmmaking knowledge.
