17. "LEARN THIS" COMMAND
Implement a natural language workflow.
User:
"Jarvis, learn this document."
JARVIS should:
1.	Identify the selected document.
2.	Upload it if necessary.
3.	Store it in Firebase Storage.
4.	Create a Firestore knowledge record.
5.	Extract its contents.
6.	Process and chunk the content.
7.	Make the knowledge searchable.
8.	Mark the document as processed.
9.	Confirm completion.
Example response:
"I've added the document to your Knowledge Library. I can now use its information when answering future questions."
Do not claim that the AI model was retrained.
________________________________________
18. KNOWLEDGE-AWARE CONVERSATIONS
JARVIS should automatically determine when stored knowledge may be relevant.
Example:
User:
"Explain three-point lighting according to what I've taught you."
JARVIS should search the Knowledge Library before answering.
Another example:
"According to my engineering project documents, what motor torque did we calculate?"
JARVIS should search the relevant project knowledge.
If no relevant knowledge is found, JARVIS should not fabricate an answer.
It should say that the information was not found in the user's stored knowledge and, where appropriate, offer to use general AI knowledge or web research.
________________________________________
19. FILE MANAGEMENT
The original application includes integration with external file services such as:
•	Google Drive
•	Mega
Preserve these capabilities.
These services should remain separate from the NAD JARVIS Knowledge Library.
External files may be browsed and managed through their respective APIs.
The user may optionally import a document from Google Drive or another connected service into the Knowledge Library.
Example:
Google Drive
     ↓
Select Document
     ↓
"Add to JARVIS Knowledge"
     ↓
Firebase Storage
     ↓
Knowledge Processing
Do not automatically copy every external file into the Knowledge Library.
Only import files when explicitly requested or configured.
________________________________________
20. SKILL SYSTEM
JARVIS must have a modular skill/tool architecture.
The AI should not directly manipulate application internals.
Instead, expose controlled skills.
Example:
calendar.create_event
calendar.find_events
calendar.update_event
calendar.delete_event

reminders.create
reminders.list
reminders.delete

memory.save
memory.search

knowledge.upload
knowledge.search
knowledge.retrieve

files.search
files.read
files.download

web.search

notifications.create
The exact list should expand as features are implemented.
Each skill should have:
•	Name
•	Description
•	Parameters
•	Execution function
•	Permission level
•	Confirmation requirement
•	Error handling
•	Logging
________________________________________
21. SKILL PERMISSIONS
Not every skill should execute automatically.
Low-risk
Examples:
•	Search knowledge
•	Search files
•	Read documents
•	Search web
•	Create drafts
•	Create reminders
Medium-risk
Examples:
•	Create calendar events
•	Modify existing data
•	Import external files
High-risk
Examples:
•	Send emails
•	Delete files
•	Delete calendar events
•	Financial actions
•	Sensitive account modifications
High-risk operations should require explicit user confirmation.
Example:
"I have prepared the email. Would you like me to send it?"
Do not silently perform irreversible or sensitive actions.
________________________________________
22. SCHEDULING AND REMINDERS
Preserve the original scheduling architecture.
JARVIS must support natural language commands such as:
"Schedule a meeting with Sarah for Friday at 3 PM."
"Show me what I have tomorrow."
"Remind me to call Sarah at 10 AM."
"Set an alarm for 6 AM."
Use:
•	Google Calendar API where appropriate
•	Firestore for application reminders
•	Firebase Cloud Functions for backend processing
•	Push notifications for alerts
________________________________________
23. VOICE INTERACTION
NAD JARVIS must remain voice-first.
Support:
Speech-to-Text
Web:
•	Web Speech API or appropriate speech service
Android:
•	Android SpeechRecognizer or appropriate speech service
Text-to-Speech
Web:
•	Browser SpeechSynthesis or appropriate TTS service
Android:
•	Android TextToSpeech or appropriate TTS service
Wake Word
Support a configurable activation phrase such as:
"Hey Jarvis"
or:
"NAD"
Wake-word functionality must respect browser/mobile platform limitations and battery/privacy constraints.
Do not falsely claim continuous wake-word functionality where the platform does not permit it.
________________________________________
24. TEXT + VOICE HYBRID MODE
The user must be able to switch naturally between:
•	Text
•	Voice
Example:
Voice:
"Jarvis, check my calendar."

↓
JARVIS responds verbally.

User then types:

"Actually, show me the schedule."

↓
JARVIS responds visually.
Conversation context must remain continuous between modes.
________________________________________
25. CONVERSATION HISTORY
Store conversation history securely in Firestore.
Conversation records should support:
•	Sessions
•	Messages
•	Timestamps
•	User/assistant roles
•	Tool calls
•	Tool results
•	Knowledge sources
•	Model used
•	Errors
•	Conversation titles
Avoid storing unnecessary sensitive information.
________________________________________
26. REAL-TIME DATABASE
Firebase Realtime Database should NOT be used as the primary knowledge database.
Use Firestore for persistent application data and knowledge metadata.
Realtime Database may be used where genuinely beneficial for:
•	Presence
•	Live session state
•	Real-time status
•	Streaming state
•	Temporary real-time synchronization
Do not duplicate persistent knowledge unnecessarily between Firestore and Realtime Database.
________________________________________
27. FIREBASE ARCHITECTURE
Use:
Firebase Authentication
For secure user authentication.
Firestore
For:
•	Conversations
•	Messages
•	Reminders
•	User preferences
•	Memory
•	Knowledge metadata
•	Knowledge chunks
•	Projects
•	Skills
•	Permissions
•	Logs
•	Application settings
Firebase Storage
For:
•	Knowledge documents
•	Uploaded files
•	User assets
•	Application files
Firebase Cloud Functions
For:
•	AI API calls
•	Tool orchestration
•	Secure backend operations
•	Document processing
•	Notifications
•	External API integration
•	Scheduled jobs
•	Knowledge processing pipelines
________________________________________
28. SECURITY
NAD JARVIS is private.
Implement strong security.
Requirements:
•	Firebase Authentication
•	Firestore security rules
•	Storage security rules
•	Server-side API calls
•	Secure environment variables
•	No API keys in frontend source code
•	HTTPS
•	Authenticated Cloud Function requests
•	User ownership validation
•	Secure file access
•	Permission-aware knowledge retrieval
Never expose:
GEMINI_API_KEY
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET
MEGA_PASSWORD
in frontend code.
All sensitive credentials must remain server-side.
________________________________________
29. KNOWLEDGE SECURITY
Knowledge retrieval must respect ownership and scope.
JARVIS must never retrieve a document merely because it matches a search query.
Before retrieval:
Authenticate user
       ↓
Verify document ownership
       ↓
Verify project/category permissions
       ↓
Retrieve allowed knowledge
       ↓
Send relevant context to AI
The knowledge system must never expose another user's data if the architecture is later expanded.
________________________________________
30. CLOUD FUNCTIONS — CENTRAL HUB
Use Firebase Cloud Functions as the secure backend orchestration hub.
Conceptually:
exports.processCommand = ...
The backend should determine whether a request requires:
General AI
Knowledge
Memory
Calendar
Reminder
File operation
Web search
Notification
Other skill
Do not create one enormous function containing every operation.
Organize backend functionality into maintainable modules.
Example:
functions/
│
├── ai/
├── knowledge/
├── memory/
├── calendar/
├── reminders/
├── files/
├── notifications/
├── integrations/
├── skills/
└── orchestrator/
________________________________________
31. KNOWLEDGE PROCESSING BACKEND
Create a dedicated knowledge-processing pipeline.
Example:
knowledge/
├── upload
├── extractText
├── parseDocument
├── chunkDocument
├── indexDocument
├── searchKnowledge
├── retrieveContext
├── deleteKnowledge
└── updateKnowledge
Processing should happen securely on the backend.
Do not rely on the browser to perform the complete knowledge ingestion pipeline.
________________________________________
32. ERROR HANDLING
Every major system must have graceful error handling.
Examples:
AI unavailable
"I'm temporarily unable to reach my AI service."
Document processing failed
"I couldn't process this document. Please check the file format and try again."
Calendar unavailable
"I couldn't access your Google Calendar."
Knowledge not found
"I couldn't find that information in your Knowledge Library."
Never fabricate successful operations.
________________________________________
